<metadata><name>McKenzie Pass Lava Fields (Highway 242 from the snow gate)</name><desc>or-vielbig-2e:258-1: route read from the book's text and sketch map. One trkseg per waypoint pair, in order; segment sources are in the trk desc. Geometry from OSM (ODbL, (c) OpenStreetMap contributors) and USFS EDW (public domain) where marked; 'straight' segments are off-trail connectors, not traced.</desc></metadata>
<wpt lat="44.307631" lon="-121.705896"><ele>1227.5</ele><name>Highway 242 snow gate</name><desc>source: DB trailhead pin th:hwy242-snow-gate = OSM node 38473692, where OSM splits OR 242 (way 1088864121 starts here); confidence: high; pdf_page: 261; book_elev_ft: 4040; quote: drive 8.4 miles west on Highway 242 to the snow gate; map 59: SNOW GATE 4040'</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="44.289700" lon="-121.763220"><ele>1490.8</ele><name>Windy Point</name><desc>source: OSM viewpoint node 3054333470 'Windy Point' (beside the road; 'Sea of Lava' information board 12 m away); 3.31 mi by road from the gate; confidence: high; pdf_page: 260; book_elev_ft: 4909; quote: Three miles from the snow gate, at Windy Point (4,909 feet), the first views of Mounts Jefferson and Washington appear</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="44.260200" lon="-121.800490"><ele>1622.8</ele><name>McKenzie Pass (Dee Wright Observatory)</name><desc>source: OSM saddle node 5373275217 'McKenzie Pass' at the end of OSM way 1088864121; Dee Wright Observatory (way 758173899) 75 m W; confidence: high; pdf_page: 260; book_elev_ft: 5324; quote: At the pass a small, unusual stone building - an observatory for summer tourists - sits on a prominent point on the south side of the road</desc></wpt>
<trk><name>McKenzie Pass Lava Fields (Highway 242 from the snow gate)</name><desc>1. Highway 242 snow gate -&gt; Windy Point: osm (high), 3.32 mi | 2. Windy Point -&gt; McKenzie Pass (Dee Wright Observatory): osm (high), 3.26 mi</desc>
